n't want no trouble  trouble ain't no trouble here during my  hands-on time with a recent demo I spent  some time tracking down a target through  the Bayou and also busting up a  prostitution ring both missions had  their own unique objectives and brought  me into the line of fire with very  different people the places to in which  I fought my battles were entertaining  and how buried in coop they were one  took place on a sinking riverboat in the  dark depths of the bayou and another  brought me into the fancier part of town  busting trapped girls out of seedy hotel  rooms these environments add two new  Bordeaux's personality and after  checking out a few I got the sense of  just how big and shady mafia three's  setting really is but while mafia  three's new Bordeaux is very similar to  New Orleans it's not quite an exact copy  the studio didn't want to lifts New  Orleans wholesale because they wanted to  keep the mafia series in fictional  cities the city of New Orleans is very  flat and there's not a lot of elevation  which means creating an entertaining  driving system would be challenging and  also a bit boring so new Bordeaux  differs from its real-world counterpart  in this with more beautiful vistas  cresting hills and less one-way streets  packed with parked cars driving felt  fluid and I enjoyed spending time in my  car listening to the radio and taking in  the sights of the city on my way to the  next hit  the other excellent thing about mafia  threes rapper is its soundtrack it's  thrilling to hear paint it black or I  fought the law as you flee the scene or  get a chance to play with the radio as  you drive through town in the dead of  night the soundtrack made up of songs  from the 1960s adds a strong sense of  time and place to mafia 3 we only have a  few short weeks left before mafia 3 hits  store shelves what are you most looking  forward to in mafia 3 let us know in the  comments and stay tuned to games.com for  more mafia news you can prey on the